    <p class="kicker">Equity mining</p>
    <h1 style="font-size:clamp(30px,5vw,50px);">Your next sale is already in your service lane.</h1>
    <p class="sub">Service customers whose car is worth more than they owe are the warmest buyers you have. The tools to spot them already exist. What's usually missing is the handoff.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<section id="loop">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">How the loop should work</p>
        <h2>Service to Sales, while the customer is still in the building.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="sub">This is the handoff our company is named for. When it works, every service visit can start the next deal.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><div class="n">01</div><h3>CHECK-IN</h3><p>A customer arrives for service. The appointment is already in your scheduler and DMS.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="n">02</div><h3>FLAG</h3><p>Their vehicle value and payoff show equity, and an alert fires before they reach the write-up.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="n">03</div><h3>HANDOFF</h3><p>The advisor and a named salesperson both see it, with a reason to talk that isn't a sales pitch.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="n">04</div><h3>OWNED</h3><p>Someone owns the follow-up, it's logged in the CRM, and the result is counted.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="n">05</div><h3>MEASURED</h3><p>Service customers who became sales are tracked month over month, from a baseline set before we start.</p></div>
      <div class="step last"><div class="n">06</div><h3>AGAIN</h3><p>The new car comes back to Service, and the loop starts over. That is the flywheel.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="goldsec" id="why-breaks">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="gold-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Why it breaks</p>
        <h2>Most stores already pay for the alert. It just doesn't reach anyone.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="gold-sub">The equity tools many stores already pay for do the math well. The loss happens after the alert fires.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="breaks">
  <div class="wrap">
    <ul class="plainlist">
      <li>The alert lands on a dashboard nobody opens during the morning service rush.</li>
      <li>Advisors aren't asked, trained or paid to make the introduction.</li>
      <li>Sales doesn't know the customer is on the lot today.</li>
      <li>The equity figure is built on a stale payoff or valuation, so nobody trusts it.</li>
      <li>Nobody measures service-to-sales, so nobody owns it.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

